<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Learning Target 4 Practice</title>

    <p>
        <em>Identify if a given set of numbers is closed under a given operation. </em>
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Need to know:</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        What it means for a set to be closed under an operation.
                        See page 19-20 of class worksheets or Fact 2.1 on page 48
                        of your textbook.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        You need to know what each of the following symbols
                        mean:
                        <m>\mathbb{N}, \mathbb{Z}, \mathbb{Q}, \mathbb{R}</m>
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        You need to be able to read sets written in
                        roster notation (so continuing the pattern of the <m>\dots</m>).
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Practice Questions (Solutions online!)</title>

        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Is <m>\mathbb{N}</m> closed under addition?
                        subtraction?
                        multiplication?
                        division?
                        Explain.  
            </p>
          </statement>

          <solution>
            <p>
                 The natural numbers are closed under addition and
                        multiplication, but not subtraction or division.
                        If <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are natural numbers, then
                        <m>a+b</m> and <m>a\cdot b</m> are natural numbers,
                        which means the natural numbers are closed under
                        addition and multiplication.
                        However, <m>3,5\in\mathbb{N}</m> and
                        <m>3-5 = -2\notin\mathbb{N}</m>, which shows the natural
                        numbers are not closed under subtraction.
                        Similarly, <m>3,5\in\mathbb{N}</m>, but
                        <m>3 \div 5 = \frac{3}{5}\notin \mathbb{N}</m>.
            </p>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                       Is <m>\mathbb{Z}</m> closed under addition?
                        subtraction?
                        multiplication?
                        division?
                        Explain.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The integers are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication, but 
                    not division.
                    The same example in the previous solution works (but changing <m>\mathbb{N}</m> to <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>). That is, <m>3,5\in\mathbb{Z}</m>, but
                        <m>3 \div 5 = \frac{3}{5}\notin \mathbb{Z}</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                       Is <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> closed under addition?
                        subtraction?
                        multiplication?
                        division?
                        Explain.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The rational numbers are closed under addition, subtraction, multiplication, but not division.
                    For example, the rational numbers are closed under multiplication because for rational numbers <m>\frac{a}{b}</m>
                    and <m>\frac{c}{d}</m> where <m>a,b,c,d\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>c,d \neq 0</m>, we have 
                    <m>\frac{a}{b}\cdot\frac{c}{d} = \frac{ab}{cd}</m>
                    and <m>ab,cd\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>cd\neq 0</m>. (See the defintion of rational numbers!)
                </p>

                <p>
                    However, the rational numbers are not closed under division because <m>0,1\in\mathbb{Q}</m> and <m>1\div 0</m> is not 
                    a rational number.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Is the set <m>A = \{2,4,6,8,\dots\}</m> closed under
                        addition?
                        subtraction?
                        multiplication?
                        division?
                        Explain.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                       The set <m>A</m> is closed under addition, since any
                        element of <m>A</m> has the form <m>2k</m> for some
                        <m>k\in\mathbb{N}</m>.
                        Let <m>a,b\in A</m>.
                        Then <m>a = 2j</m> and <m>b=2k</m> for some
                        <m>j,k\in \mathbb{N}</m>.
                        Then <m>a+b = 2j + 2k = 2(j+k)</m> and <m>j+k</m> is a
                        natural number.
                </p>

                <p>
                    However, <m>A</m> is not closed under subtraction as <m>2,6\in A</m>, but <m>2-6=-4 \notin A.</m>
                </p>
                <p>
                    The set is closed under multiplication, but not division.
                </p>
            </solution>

    
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                     Is the set <m>B = \{1,3,5,7,\dots\}</m> closed under
                        addition?
                        subtraction?
                        multiplication?
                        division?
                        Explain.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The set <m>B</m> is not closed under addition, subtraction, nor division. However, <m>B</m> is closed under
                    multiplication. We can write any element of the set <m>B</m> as <m>2k+1</m> for some natural number (or 0). 
                    Taking 2 different elements of <m>B</m>, <m>a=2k+1</m> and <m>b=2j+1</m>, we can find <m>ab</m> and show that
                    it is odd.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is the set
                        <m>C = \{\dots, -6,-2,2,6,10,14,\dots\}</m>
                        closed under addition?
                        subtraction?
                        multiplication?
                        division?
                        Explain.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                      The set
                        <m>C</m>
                        is not closed under any of these operations.
                        For examples of addition and multiplication, note
                        <m>2,6\in C</m>, but
                </p>
                 <p>
                        <m>2+6 = 8\notin C</m> as <m>8\equiv 0 \pmod{4}</m> so
                        <m>8\not\equiv 2\pmod{4}</m>
                    </p>

                     <p>
                        <m>2\cdot 6 =12\notin C</m> as
                        <m>12\equiv 0 \pmod{4}</m> so
                        <m>12\not\equiv 2\pmod{4}</m>
                    </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
      
               
    </paragraphs>

</handout>